    "knowledge_point": "Calculating an Unknown Angle",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945712/assets/95b534f8c159b37884e32b730d9aa502565ff4971cd7d08903ffbfaa94b76623.png)\n\nConsider $△PQR$ shown above. The line segment $\\overset{―}{RH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PQ}.$ If $m∠PQR={118}^{∘},$ calculate $m∠QRH.$",
    "worked_solution": "Since the straight angle must have a measure of ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HQR+m∠PQR & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HQR+{118}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HQR & ={62}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $△QHR$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QRH+m∠HQR+m∠RHQ & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH+{62}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH+{152}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH & ={28}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
